; A `br` that leaves TWO regions at once — the only shape that makes `endRegion` do work.
;
; WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS. The bridge stopped emitting `endRegion` for a region whose body cannot
; set CTL, which is nearly every region a real `.ssc` program produces: the front has no `break`, so
; nothing it lowers ever branches out past its own enclosing region. Planting an UNCONDITIONAL
; elision — drop the decrement whether or not the body diverts — left all 86 conformance fixtures
; and all 13 effects fixtures GREEN. An optimisation nothing can catch being wrong is an
; optimisation whose guard is untested, so the guard gets a fixture of its own, hand-written,
; because the front cannot write one.
;
; THE SHAPE. Inside the `if`, depth 0 is the `if` and depth 1 is the `block`, so `(br 1)` leaves
; both and sets CTL to 2. Then:
;
;   the if's endRegion    2 -> 1     <- THE INSTRUCTION UNDER TEST
;   `A` is guarded, CTL is 1         <- skipped, correctly, either way
;   the block's endRegion 1 -> 0
;   `B` runs                         <- only if the first decrement happened
;
; Drop the first decrement and CTL is still 1 when the block ends, so `B` is guarded off too and the
; program prints 10 instead of 10/20. One line of output is the whole difference, which is why it is
; the LAST print that moves rather than the first.
(module
  (consts (int 10) (int 20) (int 30) (bool true))
  (types)
  (globals)
  (prims (prim "io.println"))
  (funcs
    (func "main" 0 4
      (const 0 0)
      (prim 3 0 0)
      (block
        (const 1 3)
        (if 1
          (then (br 1))
          (else))
        (const 2 2)
        (prim 3 0 2))
      (const 0 1)
      (prim 3 0 0)
      (ret 3)))
  (entry 0))
